WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
Let me walk you through the Claude.md patterns that teams actually reach for across front-end,

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:12.239
back-end, and full-stack projects. For this video, we'll cover what goes into each one,

3
00:00:12.239 --> 00:00:17.360
a security pattern, a test pattern, and the anti-pattern that quietly undermines all of them.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.719
These are the shapes you'll meet in most files you inherit and work with every day.

5
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.479
Teams that use Claude code will rarely write a Claude.md from scratch.

6
00:00:28.559 --> 00:00:35.119
They reach for a pattern that already fits their kind of project. A Claude.md pattern is a reusable

7
00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:40.880
set of directives suited to a specific team context. Front-end patterns encode component

8
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.200
naming and state management rules. Back-end patterns encode API design and database conventions.

9
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.159
Each pattern is different because each context is different. The directive versus narrative

10
00:00:52.159 --> 00:00:58.720
distinction matters here too. A directive tells Claude code what to do. A narrative explains

11
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.440
background and rationale. Claude.md should be directives, not narrative. And tool duplication

12
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.839
is the anti-pattern of encoding rules that a linter or formatter already enforces. It wastes

13
00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:17.839
context tokens and creates conflicting sources of truth. Together, they shape how you read any

14
00:01:17.839 --> 00:01:25.040
Claude.md you inherit. The front-end pattern covers four rule categories. Component naming,

15
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.519
Pascal case for component definitions, the casing convention for file names, and co-location rules

16
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:39.360
for tests and styles. CSS methodology, whether the project uses CSS modules, Tailwind utility classes,

17
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.800
or styled components. The choice is meaningful because it affects how Claude code should

18
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.080
generate any component with styles. State management, which library is approved, whether

19
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.160
global state is permitted in certain layers, what patterns are off limits, and accessibility standards,

20
00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.000
required ARIA attributes, keyboard navigation requirements for interactive elements. None of

21
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.559
these apply to back-end code. That's exactly why a subdirectory Claude.md in the front-end folder

22
00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:14.320
is the right home for them. The back-end pattern covers the API and data layers.

23
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:21.360
API design rules encode this project's specific conventions, the rest design choices, how the team

24
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.360
version's endpoints, what the standard error response format looks like. Database query rules

25
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:34.800
encode the ORM versus raw SQL policy, any query optimization requirements, and whether direct

26
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:41.679
database access is permitted outside certain layers. Error handling rules specify which error

27
00:02:41.679 --> 00:02:48.720
types are expected to surface, how they're logged, and what format error responses take in the API.

28
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.559
These are all decisions specific to this service. Claude code can't infer them from general back-end

29
00:02:54.559 --> 00:03:01.600
knowledge. They have to be encoded. Full stack and monorepo projects need cross-cutting rules that

30
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:07.679
span multiple packages. The full stack pattern typically includes four categories. Shared type

31
00:03:07.679 --> 00:03:13.839
conventions, where TypeScript interfaces shared across front-end and back-end live, and the import

32
00:03:13.839 --> 00:03:20.160
rules for consuming them. Monorepo structure rules, which packages own which concerns, and what the

33
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.759
import restrictions are across package boundaries. Service boundary rules, what each service is

34
00:03:25.759 --> 00:03:32.240
responsible for, what data crosses service lines, and what's explicitly forbidden to cross. And

35
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.000
shared naming conventions that apply consistently everywhere in the monorepo. These rules prevent

36
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.360
the drift that develops naturally when multiple packages evolve independently without shared

37
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:50.160
guardrails in place. Security directives are among the highest value lines in any claude.md.

38
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.399
Four rules appear in nearly every security pattern. No hard-coded credentials. All secrets

39
00:03:56.399 --> 00:04:03.279
are passed via environment variables or a secrets manager, never written in line in code..env usage

40
00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:09.759
policy, which env files exist in this project, which ones are committed to version control, and

41
00:04:09.759 --> 00:04:15.759
which are never committed. Secrets management approach, which vault or service handles secrets,

42
00:04:15.759 --> 00:04:21.040
and how claude code should reference them in generated code. And authentication patterns,

43
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.239
approved libraries, forbidden patterns, and session handling rules. Security directives

44
00:04:26.239 --> 00:04:30.399
should be explicit, prominent, and placed early in the file so they're hard to miss.

45
00:04:31.679 --> 00:04:36.320
The test pattern addresses four areas where inconsistency causes real problems.

46
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.880
Test file naming, where test files live relative to source, whether they use a dot test or dot

47
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:49.040
spec suffix, and any module co-location requirements. Coverage thresholds, the minimum

48
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.920
percentages enforced in CI and any per-module targets the team has agreed to. Fixture organization,

49
00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.959
where shared fixtures live in the project and how they're imported across different test files.

50
00:05:00.959 --> 00:05:06.399
And mock policies, which external dependencies are always mocked, which should be tested with

51
00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:12.320
real implementations, and any rules around third-party service mocks. Test configuration

52
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.959
rules prevent a test suite from growing inconsistently as the code base scales.

53
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.839
The most common claude.md failure mode is writing it as a narrative document instead of a set of

54
00:05:23.839 --> 00:05:30.480
directives. A narrative claude.md has long paragraphs explaining context and rationale.

55
00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:37.600
Rules are buried inside prose. It reads like a design doc or a wiki page. Under context pressure,

56
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.119
claude code is more likely to miss rules that aren't clearly delineated. The directive pattern

57
00:05:43.119 --> 00:05:49.279
is the opposite. Bullet points one rule per line, clear headers, scannable in seconds. You can

58
00:05:49.279 --> 00:05:55.359
verify it by reading through in under a minute and confirming each directive is present and explicit.

59
00:05:55.359 --> 00:06:01.359
When you sit down to write claude.md, start with bullets. Add prose only when an instruction

60
00:06:01.359 --> 00:06:08.320
genuinely can't be understood without background context. Are you documenting a project or

61
00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:14.160
instructing a model? Onboarding prose explains why the architecture ended up the way it did.

62
00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:21.200
A directive tells claude code what to do on the next edit, and only the second one changes

63
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:28.399
behavior. That's why these patterns read as terse lists rather than paragraphs. Borrow a proven shape

64
00:06:28.399 --> 00:06:32.880
and you skip the hours of trial and error someone else already spent figuring it out.

65
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.559
That brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next one.

